{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SowmHrvV1dGUNQkffhFaYn","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text: Measuring the Discrimination Boundary\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper formalizes the concept of a generational discrimination boundary K* (the maximum depth at which adjacent generations of recursively generated text remain statistically distinguishable) and provides the first systematic empirical measurement across 9 model configurations, 4 domains, and 4 decoding strategies. Using 15 model-free statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier with a bootstrap CI threshold of theta=0.60, the authors find K* in {1,2,3}, forming a domain hierarchy: Wikipedia K*=1, C4/Code K*=2, arXiv K*=3. The paper additionally evaluates downstream filtering strategies, finding binary filtering marginally outperforms graduated depth-aware weighting, consistent with the K*=2 prediction for web text.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined operationalization.** The K* definition (Eq. 1) is clean and falsifiable: maximum k where the 95% bootstrap CI lower bound of pairwise AUC exceeds theta. The DPI grounding, while straightforward, correctly motivates the existence of a finite bound. The paper is transparent about K* being a feature-set-specific lower bound on the theoretical K*.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** 9 model configs spanning 5 architecture families (1B--14B), 4 domains, 4 decoding strategies, plus ablations on segment length, feature subsets, self-BLEU independence, base-vs-instruct, and neural probes. This is substantially more thorough than typical detection papers. The cross-model transfer matrix (Table S1), cross-scorer ANOVA (Table in Appendix), and LOFO threshold validation demonstrate unusual methodological care.\n\n- **Honest treatment of negative results and edge cases.** The paper does not hide the Mistral K*=1 exception (1/9), the Qwen-cont borderline margin (+0.15pp), the 512-token CI failure, the cross-scorer K* reductions, or the Pythia filtering null result. The Limitations section is among the most thorough I have seen, with 8 enumerated items plus an extended appendix. This transparency is commendable.\n\n- **Scorer confound resolution.** The Wikipedia scorer confound discovery (same-family scoring inflates K* from 1 to 2, resolved by cross-family scoring) is a genuine methodological contribution. The three-way ANOVA confirming Depth-Pair dominates variance (eta^2_p = 0.998) while Scorer is minor (eta^2_p = 0.192) is convincing.\n\n- **Domain hierarchy is the most interesting finding.** Mistral-7B achieving K*=1 on C4 but K*=3 on arXiv is compelling evidence that domain structure, not model identity, governs K*. This is validated across 5 models on arXiv and 6 models on Wikipedia with scorer control.\n\n- **Statistical rigor in the core K* measurement.** Permutation tests (p < 0.001 for all boundary comparisons), bootstrap CIs, GroupKFold CV grouped by seed document, power analysis showing n >= 2000 suffices, and verification that non-boundary CI upper bounds fall below theta.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Theta = 0.60 remains inadequately justified despite extensive sensitivity analysis.** The threshold is described as \"post-hoc\" and chosen at an \"empirical discontinuity.\" The LOFO validation yields theta-hat in [0.5725, 0.6004], but 0.60 sits at the extreme upper end of this range. At theta = 0.5725, the LOFO-derived value for the held-out Qwen family, Qwen-rw would be K*=3 rather than K*=2. The 5pp stability window (0.575--0.625) sounds reassuring, but the headline result K*=2 is sensitive within this very window: at theta=0.55, two Qwen configs become K*=3; at theta=0.625, OLMo drops to K*=1. The permutation null (95th percentile < 0.55) establishes that above-chance discrimination exists, but the specific cutoff between \"reliable\" and \"unreliable\" discrimination at 0.60 is still a researcher degree of freedom. The paper would benefit from reporting results at multiple theta values in the main text rather than relegating theta sensitivity entirely to appendix tables.\n\n- **Dangerously thin CI margins undermine the \"K*=2 dominant mode\" claim.** Of the 8 K*=2 configurations on C4, the CI lower bounds exceed theta by: +8.6pp (Qwen-rw), +4.5pp (Pythia), +1.7pp (Qwen-base), +1.2pp (Qwen-14B), +0.8pp (Qwen-7B), +0.45pp (OLMo), +0.28pp (LLaMA-8B), and +0.15pp (Qwen-cont). Four of eight continuation-mode models have margins below 1pp. Qwen-cont already drops to K*=1 under cross-scorer validation. LLaMA-8B has 1/5 folds below theta. A more conservative reading is that K*=2 is robust for 4/9 configs (Qwen-rw, Pythia, Qwen-base, Qwen-14B) and borderline for 4 others. The paper's rhetoric (\"8/9 yield K*=2\") does not adequately convey this fragility.\n\n- **The neural probe result (Qwen K*_neural=3 vs K*_15D=2) creates a logical tension the paper does not fully resolve.** The authors attribute the elevation to \"self-familiarity artifacts that transfer poorly,\" but this is asserted without direct evidence. An alternative interpretation is that the 15D features are an incomplete representation and the true K* for Qwen-generated C4 text is 3, not 2. The paper acknowledges K* is representation-dependent, but the main text claims center on K*=2 as if it were a property of the text rather than of the measurement apparatus. The Discussion paragraph on this is only 3 sentences long, which is insufficient for what is arguably the most challenging internal contradiction.\n\n- **Cross-scorer validation is incomplete.** Table S13 covers 7 generator configurations but does not include LLaMA-8B or Qwen-14B as generators with cross-family scorers on C4. The cross-scorer matrix in the main text (Section 4.2) states \"22 cross-scorer configurations on C4 and arXiv\" but the C4 cross-scorer matrix excludes Mistral and LLaMA as generators. Given that LLaMA-8B is the most marginal K*=2 case (+0.28pp margin), omitting its cross-scorer validation is a significant gap. If LLaMA-8B drops to K*=1 under cross-scoring (as Qwen-cont does), the headline \"8/9 K*=2\" becomes \"6/9 K*=2.\"\n\n- **C3 (filtering contribution) is weak.** The binary filtering advantage is +0.76pp MMLU in the 100% synthetic setting, but: (a) the absolute gap from base model is only -0.77pp (binary) vs -1.53pp (no-filter), meaning the entire dynamic range is ~0.8pp; (b) Pythia replication shows all strategies within noise (max delta = 0.17pp); (c) arXiv filtering despite K*=3 shows binary *hurts* MMLU by -0.23pp (p=0.005) while graduated is indistinguishable from no-filter; (d) dose-response shows synthetic data is net-beneficial at realistic contamination ratios (10-50%), making filtering unnecessary. The arXiv result is particularly damaging: K*=3 should provide *more* granularity for graduated filtering, yet it fails. The paper does not adequately explain why higher K* does not translate to better graduated filtering. Claiming \"K*=2 correctly predicts binary filtering as optimal granularity\" when the arXiv K*=3 case contradicts this logic weakens the contribution.\n\n- **The retrain-chain K* gap (2 vs 7) raises serious ecological validity concerns.** Real-world contamination involves both prompting and retraining. The 3.5x gap (K*=2 prompt-chain vs K*=7 retrain-chain for both Qwen and Pythia) means the prompt-chain K* measured in this paper may be largely irrelevant to the actual contamination setting practitioners care about. The paper acknowledges this in limitations (\"prompt-chain K* may underestimate discriminability in mixed settings\") but the main framing still treats prompt-chain K*=2 as the actionable result. This disconnect between the controlled setting and real-world applicability needs more prominent discussion.\n\n- **512-token borderline failure threatens ecological validity.** At 512 tokens (closer to real document lengths), CI lower = 0.5979 < theta, making K* borderline. The paper's main experiments use 256-token segments, but real web documents are typically longer. The authors do not explain *why* longer segments reduce discriminability (one would expect more tokens to provide more signal). This counterintuitive result is discussed in only one sentence in the Limitations section.\n\n- **Qwen family overrepresentation.** 5/9 configurations are Qwen variants (1.5B-Instruct rewrite, 1.5B-Instruct continuation, 1.5B base, 7B, 14B). While spanning different scales and training variants, this means aggregate statistics are heavily weighted toward one family. The paper acknowledges this (Limitation 8) but the main text framing (\"5 architecture families\") somewhat obscures that Qwen contributes the majority of data points.\n\n- **Scale trend beyond 14B is unaddressed.** The Discussion notes that \"larger models produce more human-like text, reducing 0v1 JSD\" (0.16-0.29 at <=1.5B, 0.077-0.13 at 7-14B) and predicts \"K* may decrease as capability scales beyond 14B.\" The 32B results are only preliminary ranges in the dream report version. Given that frontier models are 100B+, the paper's findings may not generalize to the models that matter most for real contamination concerns. The monotonic decrease in 0v1 AUC from 0.997 (1.5B) to 0.922 (8B) to 0.960 (14B, slight uptick but still lower than small models) suggests the boundary will eventually compress.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Theta justification**: Can you provide a principled, non-post-hoc criterion for theta? The LOFO-derived range [0.5725, 0.6004] suggests theta=0.59 might be equally defensible, which would change the Qwen-rw result from K*=2 to K*=3 and alter the narrative. How do you respond to the concern that theta=0.60 was selected because it produces the cleanest story?\n\n2. **LLaMA-8B cross-scorer**: Have you run cross-family scorer validation for LLaMA-8B on C4? Given its +0.28pp margin (the thinnest among non-Qwen-cont configs), does it survive cross-scoring?\n\n3. **Neural probe tension**: You attribute Qwen's K*_neural=3 to \"self-familiarity artifacts.\" Can you provide evidence for this? For instance, does a Qwen neural probe trained on Qwen text and evaluated on Pythia text still show K*=3? If the elevation is truly self-familiarity, it should vanish under cross-model evaluation.\n\n4. **512-token paradox**: Why does discriminability *decrease* at 512 tokens? More tokens should provide more statistical signal. Is this an artifact of the generation protocol (e.g., longer generations converge faster)?\n\n5. **ArXiv filtering failure**: K*=3 means three levels of reliable depth discrimination exist for arXiv. Why does graduated filtering fail to exploit this? Does the OBD classifier's depth prediction accuracy at depths 2-3 (which should be separable for arXiv) degrade in the filtering pipeline?\n\n6. **Retrain-chain practical implications**: Given that K*=7 under retraining (both Qwen and Pythia), and real contamination involves mixed prompt+retrain chains, what is the practical recommendation? Should practitioners assume K*=2 or K*=7?\n\n7. **Cohen's d paradox in filtering**: You report Cohen's d > 8 for some filtering comparisons but absolute deltas < 1pp. This suggests the variance is extremely small (5 seeds), raising the question: are the \"significant\" differences practically meaningful? Would 20 seeds produce the same ordering?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The Conclusion (Section 7) is a single sentence. For a paper of this complexity, a 3-4 sentence conclusion summarizing key takeaways and implications would be more appropriate.\n\n- Table 1 (main text) shows only 5 of 9 configurations. The extended table is in the appendix. For the main claim \"8/9 yield K*=2,\" the main table should show all 9 or at least summarize them.\n\n- The paper title in the main .tex file (\"Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text: Measuring the Discrimination Boundary\") differs from the dream report version (\"Bounded Generational Discrimination in Recursive Synthetic Text: K* Saturates Within Three Passes\"). The former is more accurate; the latter overstates by implying K* universally saturates within three passes (arXiv retrain-chain K*=7 contradicts this).\n\n- Section 6 (Discussion) has 7 paragraph labels sharing the same \\label command block (lines 3-8 of discussion.tex). This is a LaTeX antipattern that will cause cross-reference issues.\n\n- The paper uses \"largely generator-independent\" repeatedly. The qualifier \"largely\" is doing significant work given that Qwen-rewrite transfers at only 61-63%.\n\n- arXiv sample size (n=2,200 vs n=5,000 for C4) reduces statistical power. The subsampling control showing C4 at n=2,200 drops to K*=1 (from K*=2 at n=5,000) raises concern about whether arXiv K*=3 might be K*=4 at n=5,000.\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.5 (Borderline Conference / Findings)**\n\nThe paper addresses a genuinely novel question (at what generational depth does discrimination become impossible?) with commendable experimental thoroughness and statistical rigor. The domain hierarchy finding (Wikipedia < C4/Code < arXiv) is interesting and well-supported. The scorer confound discovery and resolution is a real methodological contribution.\n\nHowever, several issues prevent a confident acceptance recommendation. First, the thin CI margins for the headline K*=2 claim (4/8 continuation-mode configs have margins < 1pp, one already fails cross-scoring) mean the central result is more fragile than presented. Second, the neural probe contradiction (Qwen K*_neural=3 vs K*_15D=2) is inadequately addressed and undermines the claim that K*=2 is text-intrinsic. Third, the filtering contribution (C3) is almost entirely negative: graduated filtering fails everywhere, binary filtering helps by < 1pp on one model and is within noise on another, and the arXiv case contradicts the narrative that K* predicts filtering granularity. Fourth, the retrain-chain gap (K*=2 vs K*=7) and the lack of scale results beyond 14B limit practical relevance.\n\nThe paper would be strengthened by: (a) more forthright framing of margin fragility in the main text, (b) resolving the neural probe tension with cross-model probe experiments, (c) either strengthening C3 with a positive arXiv graduated result or reframing it as a pure negative finding with appropriate narrative adjustment, and (d) completing cross-scorer validation for all 9 configs.\n\nAs it stands, the measurement contribution (C1+C2) is solid enough for Findings, but the fragility of the headline number and the weak/contradictory filtering results prevent a confident main-conference recommendation.\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High).** I have read the full paper including all appendix tables and ablations. I am familiar with the model collapse, synthetic text detection, and data curation literatures. My main uncertainty is whether the theta sensitivity and CI margin concerns are as damaging as I assess, or whether the community will accept the LOFO validation as sufficient justification."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af3bd492ac5381e6e (use SendMessage with to: 'af3bd492ac5381e6e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 89232\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 158822</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ae90b1b-19b4-4021-a8d9-15784d0863dc","uuid":"19f4dbef-76ca-44e5-9b40-717b5029a1d4","timestamp":"2026-05-19T10:13:20.535Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **methodology soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Evaluate against a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Bounded Generational Discrimination in Recursive Synthetic Text: K* Saturates Within Three Passes\"\n\nThe paper introduces *generational discrimination depth* K*, defined as the maximum generation depth at which pairwise statistical discrimination (via AUC of a LightGBM classifier on 15D text features) remains reliable above a calibrated threshold θ=0.60. Using the data processing inequality (DPI), K* is guaranteed finite. The core empirical finding: K* ∈ {1,2,3} across all tested conditions (9 model configs, 4 domains, 4 decoding strategies).\n\n**Key results by domain:**\n- Wikipedia: K*=1 (6/6 models under cross-family scorer control)\n- C4 web text: K*=2 (8/9 configs; Mistral-7B is K*=1)\n- arXiv: K*=3 (5/5 domain-competent models)\n- Code/Python: K*=2 for 2/5 models, K*=1 for 3/5 (model-dependent)\n\n**Three contributions claimed:**\n1. (C1) Formalize K* with DPI grounding + first systematic measurement across 9 models, 4 domains, 4 decoding strategies\n2. (C2) Domain hierarchy: Wikipedia K*=1, C4/Code K*∈{1,2}, arXiv K*=3\n3. (C3) Downstream filtering evaluation: binary filtering helps (+0.76pp MMLU), graduated depth-aware weighting provides zero additional benefit\n\n## Method Details\n\n- **Depth chain generation:** Source corpus D_0 → apply LLM → D_1 → ... → D_K (depth 0-5). Two modes: continuation (canonical) and rewrite (ablation only).\n- **Features:** 15D model-free features: surprisal (6D), entropy (4D), distributional (5D). Selected for cross-model universality.\n- **K* determination:** For each (k, k+1) pair, train LightGBM binary classifier, compute 5-fold CV AUC, require 95% bootstrap CI lower bound > θ=0.60.\n- **Filtering strategies:** No-filter, Binary (remove all synthetic), Graduated (depth-aware weighting with α parameter).\n\n## Key Experimental Data\n\n**Table 1 - C4 K* (9 configs):**\n| Model | Mode | AUC 0v1 | AUC 1v2 | AUC 2v3 | CI_1v2 lower | K* |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Qwen-1.5B-Inst | rewrite | 0.9956 | 0.6957 | 0.5893 | 0.6859 | 2 |\n| Pythia-1.4B | cont | 0.9932 | 0.6539 | 0.5515 | 0.6454 | 2 |\n| OLMo-1B | cont | 0.9970 | 0.6146 | 0.5497 | 0.6045 | 2 |\n| Qwen-7B | cont | 0.9644 | 0.6178 | 0.5460 | 0.6079 | 2 |\n| LLaMA-8B | cont | 0.9225 | 0.6123 | 0.5274 | 0.6028 | 2 |\n| Qwen-1.5B-Inst | cont | 0.9972 | 0.6114 | 0.5609 | 0.6015 | 2 |\n| Qwen-1.5B base | cont | 0.9870 | 0.6262 | 0.5496 | 0.6168 | 2 |\n| Mistral-7B | cont | 0.9843 | 0.5935 | 0.5402 | 0.5835 | 1 |\n\n**θ-Sensitivity (C4 K*=2 count out of 9):**\n- θ=0.55: 9/9\n- θ=0.60: 8/9 (Qwen-cont at +0.15pp margin)\n- θ=0.65: ~5/9\n- θ=0.70: ~2/9\n\n**Cross-scorer K* matrix (C4, 4 gen × 4 scorer):** 11/12 K*=2, one exception = Qwen-cont scored by Pythia (1v2=0.5979, K*=1). Does NOT include Mistral/LLaMA as generators.\n\n**Text length ablation:** 128→K*=2, 256→K*=2, 512→borderline (CI lower=0.5979 < θ).\n\n**Neural probe:** Pythia/OLMo K*_neural=2 (agrees with 15D), Qwen K*_neural=3 (exceeds 15D).\n\n**Retrain chain:** Pythia K*≥4, Qwen K*=7 (vs prompt-chain K*=2). 3.5× gap.\n\n**Scale trend (Qwen family on C4):**\n- 1.5B: 0v1=0.9972, 1v2=0.6114, K*=2\n- 7B: 0v1=0.9644, 1v2=0.6178, K*=2\n- 14B: 0v1=0.9598, 1v2=0.6210, K*=2\n- 32B (preliminary): 0v1=0.90-0.94, 1v2=0.58-0.62, K*=1-2\n\n**C3 Downstream Filtering (C4, Qwen-1.5B, 30% contamination):**\n- No filter: MMLU 0.5865±0.0010\n- Binary-ours: MMLU 0.5941±0.0008 (best, +0.76pp)\n- Graduated (α=0.9): MMLU 0.5892±0.0008\n- Base model (no CPT): MMLU 0.6018\n\n**Dose-response (10/30/50%):** Binary advantage significant at all ratios. Graduated = no-filter at ALL ratios.\n\n**Pythia filtering replication:** All strategies within noise (Δ_max=0.17pp). Negative result.\n\n**arXiv filtering:** Despite K*=3, all strategies identical. Most damaging negative result for C3.\n\n**Statistical tests:** Holm-Bonferroni corrected: 8/15 MMLU, 9/15 HellaSwag significant pairs. Graduated vs DSIR p=0.0523 (n.s.). Cohen's d > 8 but absolute Δ < 1pp.\n\n**OBD vs LLM McNemar:** OBD 38.0% vs GPT-5.5 33.2% (p=0.081) vs Claude 33.1% (p=0.069). Neither significant.\n\n**Missing experiment:** Naturalistic validation (Common Crawl) acknowledged as not done.\n\n## Experiments from design document NOT fully covered in paper:\n- Qwen-32B: only preliminary range estimates (Table 5, marked ‡)\n- Code domain Mistral-7B and Qwen-7B: only worst-case predictions (Table 4, marked ‡)\n- Naturalistic validation (Common Crawl): explicitly acknowledged as missing in limitations\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, with evidence from the paper)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific evidence/numbers)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nFocus on:\n- Is the methodology (15D features + LightGBM + bootstrap CI + θ threshold) sound?\n- Is θ=0.60 well-justified or arbitrary? How sensitive are claims to this choice?\n- Are the CI margins sufficient? (Several models at +0.15pp to +0.45pp above θ)\n- Does the neural probe result (Qwen K*_neural=3 vs K*_15D=2) undermine the claim that K*=2 is data-intrinsic?\n- Is the cross-scorer validation complete? (Missing Mistral/LLaMA as generators)\n- Does the 512-token borderline failure threaten ecological validity?\n- Is C3 a meaningful contribution given it's entirely negative?\n- Does the retrain chain K* gap (2 vs 7) undermine generalizability?\n- How concerning is the scale trend (32B borderline)?\n- Statistical rigor of filtering experiments (high Cohen's d but tiny absolute Δ)\n\nBe thorough and critical but fair. Cite specific numbers from the paper.","agentId":"af3bd492ac5381e6e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text: Measuring the Discrimination Boundary\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper formalizes the concept of a generational discrimination boundary K* (the maximum depth at which adjacent generations of recursively generated text remain statistically distinguishable) and provides the first systematic empirical measurement across 9 model configurations, 4 domains, and 4 decoding strategies. Using 15 model-free statistical features and an OBD-LightGBM classifier with a bootstrap CI threshold of theta=0.60, the authors find K* in {1,2,3}, forming a domain hierarchy: Wikipedia K*=1, C4/Code K*=2, arXiv K*=3. The paper additionally evaluates downstream filtering strategies, finding binary filtering marginally outperforms graduated depth-aware weighting, consistent with the K*=2 prediction for web text.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined operationalization.** The K* definition (Eq. 1) is clean and falsifiable: maximum k where the 95% bootstrap CI lower bound of pairwise AUC exceeds theta. The DPI grounding, while straightforward, correctly motivates the existence of a finite bound. The paper is transparent about K* being a feature-set-specific lower bound on the theoretical K*.\n\n- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** 9 model configs spanning 5 architecture families (1B--14B), 4 domains, 4 decoding strategies, plus ablations on segment length, feature subsets, self-BLEU independence, base-vs-instruct, and neural probes. This is substantially more thorough than typical detection papers. The cross-model transfer matrix (Table S1), cross-scorer ANOVA (Table in Appendix), and LOFO threshold validation demonstrate unusual methodological care.\n\n- **Honest treatment of negative results and edge cases.** The paper does not hide the Mistral K*=1 exception (1/9), the Qwen-cont borderline margin (+0.15pp), the 512-token CI failure, the cross-scorer K* reductions, or the Pythia filtering null result. The Limitations section is among the most thorough I have seen, with 8 enumerated items plus an extended appendix. This transparency is commendable.\n\n- **Scorer confound resolution.** The Wikipedia scorer confound discovery (same-family scoring inflates K* from 1 to 2, resolved by cross-family scoring) is a genuine methodological contribution. The three-way ANOVA confirming Depth-Pair dominates variance (eta^2_p = 0.998) while Scorer is minor (eta^2_p = 0.192) is convincing.\n\n- **Domain hierarchy is the most interesting finding.** Mistral-7B achieving K*=1 on C4 but K*=3 on arXiv is compelling evidence that domain structure, not model identity, governs K*. This is validated across 5 models on arXiv and 6 models on Wikipedia with scorer control.\n\n- **Statistical rigor in the core K* measurement.** Permutation tests (p < 0.001 for all boundary comparisons), bootstrap CIs, GroupKFold CV grouped by seed document, power analysis showing n >= 2000 suffices, and verification that non-boundary CI upper bounds fall below theta.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **Theta = 0.60 remains inadequately justified despite extensive sensitivity analysis.** The threshold is described as \"post-hoc\" and chosen at an \"empirical discontinuity.\" The LOFO validation yields theta-hat in [0.5725, 0.6004], but 0.60 sits at the extreme upper end of this range. At theta = 0.5725, the LOFO-derived value for the held-out Qwen family, Qwen-rw would be K*=3 rather than K*=2. The 5pp stability window (0.575--0.625) sounds reassuring, but the headline result K*=2 is sensitive within this very window: at theta=0.55, two Qwen configs become K*=3; at theta=0.625, OLMo drops to K*=1. The permutation null (95th percentile < 0.55) establishes that above-chance discrimination exists, but the specific cutoff between \"reliable\" and \"unreliable\" discrimination at 0.60 is still a researcher degree of freedom. The paper would benefit from reporting results at multiple theta values in the main text rather than relegating theta sensitivity entirely to appendix tables.\n\n- **Dangerously thin CI margins undermine the \"K*=2 dominant mode\" claim.** Of the 8 K*=2 configurations on C4, the CI lower bounds exceed theta by: +8.6pp (Qwen-rw), +4.5pp (Pythia), +1.7pp (Qwen-base), +1.2pp (Qwen-14B), +0.8pp (Qwen-7B), +0.45pp (OLMo), +0.28pp (LLaMA-8B), and +0.15pp (Qwen-cont). Four of eight continuation-mode models have margins below 1pp. Qwen-cont already drops to K*=1 under cross-scorer validation. LLaMA-8B has 1/5 folds below theta. A more conservative reading is that K*=2 is robust for 4/9 configs (Qwen-rw, Pythia, Qwen-base, Qwen-14B) and borderline for 4 others. The paper's rhetoric (\"8/9 yield K*=2\") does not adequately convey this fragility.\n\n- **The neural probe result (Qwen K*_neural=3 vs K*_15D=2) creates a logical tension the paper does not fully resolve.** The authors attribute the elevation to \"self-familiarity artifacts that transfer poorly,\" but this is asserted without direct evidence. An alternative interpretation is that the 15D features are an incomplete representation and the true K* for Qwen-generated C4 text is 3, not 2. The paper acknowledges K* is representation-dependent, but the main text claims center on K*=2 as if it were a property of the text rather than of the measurement apparatus. The Discussion paragraph on this is only 3 sentences long, which is insufficient for what is arguably the most challenging internal contradiction.\n\n- **Cross-scorer validation is incomplete.** Table S13 covers 7 generator configurations but does not include LLaMA-8B or Qwen-14B as generators with cross-family scorers on C4. The cross-scorer matrix in the main text (Section 4.2) states \"22 cross-scorer configurations on C4 and arXiv\" but the C4 cross-scorer matrix excludes Mistral and LLaMA as generators. Given that LLaMA-8B is the most marginal K*=2 case (+0.28pp margin), omitting its cross-scorer validation is a significant gap. If LLaMA-8B drops to K*=1 under cross-scoring (as Qwen-cont does), the headline \"8/9 K*=2\" becomes \"6/9 K*=2.\"\n\n- **C3 (filtering contribution) is weak.** The binary filtering advantage is +0.76pp MMLU in the 100% synthetic setting, but: (a) the absolute gap from base model is only -0.77pp (binary) vs -1.53pp (no-filter), meaning the entire dynamic range is ~0.8pp; (b) Pythia replication shows all strategies within noise (max delta = 0.17pp); (c) arXiv filtering despite K*=3 shows binary *hurts* MMLU by -0.23pp (p=0.005) while graduated is indistinguishable from no-filter; (d) dose-response shows synthetic data is net-beneficial at realistic contamination ratios (10-50%), making filtering unnecessary. The arXiv result is particularly damaging: K*=3 should provide *more* granularity for graduated filtering, yet it fails. The paper does not adequately explain why higher K* does not translate to better graduated filtering. Claiming \"K*=2 correctly predicts binary filtering as optimal granularity\" when the arXiv K*=3 case contradicts this logic weakens the contribution.\n\n- **The retrain-chain K* gap (2 vs 7) raises serious ecological validity concerns.** Real-world contamination involves both prompting and retraining. The 3.5x gap (K*=2 prompt-chain vs K*=7 retrain-chain for both Qwen and Pythia) means the prompt-chain K* measured in this paper may be largely irrelevant to the actual contamination setting practitioners care about. The paper acknowledges this in limitations (\"prompt-chain K* may underestimate discriminability in mixed settings\") but the main framing still treats prompt-chain K*=2 as the actionable result. This disconnect between the controlled setting and real-world applicability needs more prominent discussion.\n\n- **512-token borderline failure threatens ecological validity.** At 512 tokens (closer to real document lengths), CI lower = 0.5979 < theta, making K* borderline. The paper's main experiments use 256-token segments, but real web documents are typically longer. The authors do not explain *why* longer segments reduce discriminability (one would expect more tokens to provide more signal). This counterintuitive result is discussed in only one sentence in the Limitations section.\n\n- **Qwen family overrepresentation.** 5/9 configurations are Qwen variants (1.5B-Instruct rewrite, 1.5B-Instruct continuation, 1.5B base, 7B, 14B). While spanning different scales and training variants, this means aggregate statistics are heavily weighted toward one family. The paper acknowledges this (Limitation 8) but the main text framing (\"5 architecture families\") somewhat obscures that Qwen contributes the majority of data points.\n\n- **Scale trend beyond 14B is unaddressed.** The Discussion notes that \"larger models produce more human-like text, reducing 0v1 JSD\" (0.16-0.29 at <=1.5B, 0.077-0.13 at 7-14B) and predicts \"K* may decrease as capability scales beyond 14B.\" The 32B results are only preliminary ranges in the dream report version. Given that frontier models are 100B+, the paper's findings may not generalize to the models that matter most for real contamination concerns. The monotonic decrease in 0v1 AUC from 0.997 (1.5B) to 0.922 (8B) to 0.960 (14B, slight uptick but still lower than small models) suggests the boundary will eventually compress.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Theta justification**: Can you provide a principled, non-post-hoc criterion for theta? The LOFO-derived range [0.5725, 0.6004] suggests theta=0.59 might be equally defensible, which would change the Qwen-rw result from K*=2 to K*=3 and alter the narrative. How do you respond to the concern that theta=0.60 was selected because it produces the cleanest story?\n\n2. **LLaMA-8B cross-scorer**: Have you run cross-family scorer validation for LLaMA-8B on C4? Given its +0.28pp margin (the thinnest among non-Qwen-cont configs), does it survive cross-scoring?\n\n3. **Neural probe tension**: You attribute Qwen's K*_neural=3 to \"self-familiarity artifacts.\" Can you provide evidence for this? For instance, does a Qwen neural probe trained on Qwen text and evaluated on Pythia text still show K*=3? If the elevation is truly self-familiarity, it should vanish under cross-model evaluation.\n\n4. **512-token paradox**: Why does discriminability *decrease* at 512 tokens? More tokens should provide more statistical signal. Is this an artifact of the generation protocol (e.g., longer generations converge faster)?\n\n5. **ArXiv filtering failure**: K*=3 means three levels of reliable depth discrimination exist for arXiv. Why does graduated filtering fail to exploit this? Does the OBD classifier's depth prediction accuracy at depths 2-3 (which should be separable for arXiv) degrade in the filtering pipeline?\n\n6. **Retrain-chain practical implications**: Given that K*=7 under retraining (both Qwen and Pythia), and real contamination involves mixed prompt+retrain chains, what is the practical recommendation? Should practitioners assume K*=2 or K*=7?\n\n7. **Cohen's d paradox in filtering**: You report Cohen's d > 8 for some filtering comparisons but absolute deltas < 1pp. This suggests the variance is extremely small (5 seeds), raising the question: are the \"significant\" differences practically meaningful? Would 20 seeds produce the same ordering?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The Conclusion (Section 7) is a single sentence. For a paper of this complexity, a 3-4 sentence conclusion summarizing key takeaways and implications would be more appropriate.\n\n- Table 1 (main text) shows only 5 of 9 configurations. The extended table is in the appendix. For the main claim \"8/9 yield K*=2,\" the main table should show all 9 or at least summarize them.\n\n- The paper title in the main .tex file (\"Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text: Measuring the Discrimination Boundary\") differs from the dream report version (\"Bounded Generational Discrimination in Recursive Synthetic Text: K* Saturates Within Three Passes\"). The former is more accurate; the latter overstates by implying K* universally saturates within three passes (arXiv retrain-chain K*=7 contradicts this).\n\n- Section 6 (Discussion) has 7 paragraph labels sharing the same \\label command block (lines 3-8 of discussion.tex). This is a LaTeX antipattern that will cause cross-reference issues.\n\n- The paper uses \"largely generator-independent\" repeatedly. The qualifier \"largely\" is doing significant work given that Qwen-rewrite transfers at only 61-63%.\n\n- arXiv sample size (n=2,200 vs n=5,000 for C4) reduces statistical power. The subsampling control showing C4 at n=2,200 drops to K*=1 (from K*=2 at n=5,000) raises concern about whether arXiv K*=3 might be K*=4 at n=5,000.\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.5 (Borderline Conference / Findings)**\n\nThe paper addresses a genuinely novel question (at what generational depth does discrimination become impossible?) with commendable experimental thoroughness and statistical rigor. The domain hierarchy finding (Wikipedia < C4/Code < arXiv) is interesting and well-supported. The scorer confound discovery and resolution is a real methodological contribution.\n\nHowever, several issues prevent a confident acceptance recommendation. First, the thin CI margins for the headline K*=2 claim (4/8 continuation-mode configs have margins < 1pp, one already fails cross-scoring) mean the central result is more fragile than presented. Second, the neural probe contradiction (Qwen K*_neural=3 vs K*_15D=2) is inadequately addressed and undermines the claim that K*=2 is text-intrinsic. Third, the filtering contribution (C3) is almost entirely negative: graduated filtering fails everywhere, binary filtering helps by < 1pp on one model and is within noise on another, and the arXiv case contradicts the narrative that K* predicts filtering granularity. Fourth, the retrain-chain gap (K*=2 vs K*=7) and the lack of scale results beyond 14B limit practical relevance.\n\nThe paper would be strengthened by: (a) more forthright framing of margin fragility in the main text, (b) resolving the neural probe tension with cross-model probe experiments, (c) either strengthening C3 with a positive arXiv graduated result or reframing it as a pure negative finding with appropriate narrative adjustment, and (d) completing cross-scorer validation for all 9 configs.\n\nAs it stands, the measurement contribution (C1+C2) is solid enough for Findings, but the fragility of the headline number and the weak/contradictory filtering results prevent a confident main-conference recommendation.\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4/5 (High).** I have read the full paper including all appendix tables and ablations. I am familiar with the model collapse, synthetic text detection, and data curation literatures. My main uncertainty is whether the theta sensitivity and CI margin concerns are as damaging as I assess, or whether the community will accept the LOFO validation as sufficient justification."}],"totalDurationMs":158822,"totalTokens":89232,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":690,"cache_read_input_tokens":84532,"output_tokens":4007,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":690},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4007,"cache_read_input_tokens":84532,"cache_creation_input_tokens":690,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":690,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":14,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":4}}}
