{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EQYdjJR7AegLfmYPuZnseS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text: Measuring the Discrimination Boundary\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Focus: Novelty/Significance and Related Work Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper formalizes the concept of a generational discrimination boundary K* (the maximum recursive generation depth at which adjacent generations remain statistically distinguishable) and measures it empirically using 15 hand-crafted statistical features and LightGBM classifiers. Across 9 model configurations (5 architecture families, 1B--14B), 4 domains, and 4 decoding strategies, the paper finds K* in {1, 2, 3}, forming a domain hierarchy: Wikipedia K*=1, C4/Code K*=2, arXiv K*=3. A downstream filtering experiment shows binary filtering marginally outperforms graduated depth-aware weighting, which the authors interpret as consistent with K*=2 predicting binary granularity as optimal. The paper is thorough in its ablations and commendably transparent about negative results and boundary fragility.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Systematic empirical coverage.** The experimental matrix is impressively comprehensive: 9 model configurations, 4 domains, 4 decoding strategies, cross-scorer validation, neural probes, retrain-chain comparisons, dose-response analysis, feature ablations, and power analysis. This is one of the most thorough empirical studies I have seen on recursive generation phenomena. The 77+ experiments reflect genuine scientific diligence.\n\n- **Intellectual honesty.** The paper is unusually forthright about negative results: graduated filtering provides zero benefit, cross-scorer validation drops one configuration below threshold, the Pythia replication shows negligible effects, arXiv filtering is negative, Code domain shows model-dependent variation, and 512-token segments fail CI tests. This honesty strengthens credibility and is rare in submitted manuscripts.\n\n- **Well-defined operational metric.** The formalization of K* via Eq. 1 (CI-based threshold criterion) is clean and reproducible. The leave-one-family-out threshold validation (Table in Appendix) is a thoughtful methodological addition that partially addresses threshold arbitrariness concerns.\n\n- **Domain hierarchy as organizing principle.** The finding that domain structural complexity (Wikipedia < C4/Code < arXiv) governs K* more than model identity or scale is a useful empirical observation. The Mistral C4 K*=1 vs. arXiv K*=3 contrast is the paper's most compelling data point for domain dependence.\n\n- **Cross-scorer validation.** The ANOVA decomposition (Table in Appendix) showing Depth-Pair dominates variance (eta-squared = 0.998) while Scorer is minor (0.192) is a well-designed control that addresses a real confound.\n\n- **Related work is now reasonably comprehensive.** The bibliography contains approximately 40 references covering model collapse, binary detection, data selection, ordinal classification, watermarking, paraphrase robustness, and beyond-binary detection. This is adequate for the scope.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The core finding (K* <= 3) has limited novelty beyond what model collapse theory already implies.**\n\nThe model collapse literature (Shumailov et al. 2024, Dohmatob et al. 2024, Alemohammad et al. 2024) already establishes that recursive generation converges rapidly, with tail modes vanishing within a few iterations. Kazdan et al. (2025) show covariance collapse to zero within 5--10 iterations in the replace regime. Shi et al. (2025) show GS drops approximately exponentially. The paper's own JSD analysis (Figure in Discussion) shows the same exponential decay pattern. Given this prior knowledge, the finding that a 15D LightGBM classifier cannot distinguish generations beyond depth 2--3 is not surprising; it would be surprising if K* were large. The DPI argument is invoked as \"theoretical grounding,\" but DPI merely guarantees monotonic information loss in any Markov chain, which is trivially true. The paper acknowledges this (\"DPI guarantees K* is finite\") but does not derive any non-trivial bound from theory. The gap between \"DPI says K* exists\" and \"we measure K* = 2\" is filled entirely by empirical measurement, not by theory.\n\n**W2. The downstream utility of K* is essentially zero, undermining the practical motivation.**\n\nThe paper's Introduction frames K* as relevant to data curation: \"the saturation point determines filtering granularity.\" However:\n- Graduated filtering provides zero benefit over binary at ALL tested conditions (C4, arXiv, all contamination levels 10--50%, both Qwen and Pythia). This is the paper's own finding, stated explicitly.\n- Binary filtering's advantage over no-filter is 0.76pp MMLU on Qwen (Table in Section 5). On Pythia, the advantage is 0.17pp MMLU, within noise.\n- All CPT conditions degrade vs. the base model (base MMLU = 0.6018; best filtered = 0.5941). The filtering does not recover base model performance.\n- ArXiv filtering (where K*=3 should matter most) shows no benefit for any strategy.\n- The \"K* predicts optimal granularity\" claim reduces to: \"binary is better than graduated\" (true) and \"binary is better than nothing\" (marginally true, 0.76pp). But binary detection for data curation was already demonstrated by Drayson et al. (2025). K* adds no actionable information beyond what binary detection already provides.\n\n**W3. The 15D feature set is standard and the classifier is off-the-shelf; there is no methodological contribution.**\n\nThe 15 features (surprisal moments, derivatives, TTR, hapax ratio, self-BLEU, frequency statistics) are well-known text quality metrics used in MGT detection since at least 2019 (GLTR). DivEye shares 4 of these features. The classifier is LightGBM with default-like hyperparameters. The OBD decomposition follows Frank & Hall (2001). The paper correctly frames its contribution as \"problem formulation + empirical characterization\" rather than methodology, but this means the technical novelty rests entirely on the K* concept and its measurement, which loops back to W1.\n\n**W4. K*=2 is fragile and threshold-dependent, weakening the headline claim.**\n\nThe headline \"K*=2 for web text\" is sensitive to multiple factors:\n- **Threshold sensitivity:** At theta=0.65, roughly 5/9 C4 configurations retain K*=2; at theta=0.70, all drop to K*=1 (Figure 3). The stable window is only [0.575, 0.625], a 5pp range.\n- **Cross-scorer sensitivity:** Qwen-cont drops from K*=2 to K*=1 under cross-family scoring (Table in Appendix). The CI lower bound exceeds theta by only 0.2pp (0.602 vs. 0.600).\n- **Sample size sensitivity:** At n=2200 (matching arXiv), C4 drops to K*=1 (Appendix).\n- **Length sensitivity:** 512-token segments produce CI failure (lower bound 0.5979 < 0.60).\n- **Scale trend:** 0v1 AUC declines from >0.99 at 1.5B to 0.922 at 8B, suggesting K* may decrease further at larger scales. The paper acknowledges this but has no data beyond 14B.\n\nThe boundary between K*=1 and K*=2 for web text is essentially a coin flip at the margins. Many of the \"K*=2\" assignments survive by 0.2--2.8pp above theta. This fragility undermines the claim that K*=2 is a robust, generalizable finding rather than a measurement artifact of the specific theta, sample size, and feature set.\n\n**W5. The Code domain results are incomplete and weaken the claimed hierarchy.**\n\nThe domain hierarchy (Wiki=1, C4=Code=2, arXiv=3) is clean only for Wikipedia and arXiv. Code shows: Qwen-1.5B K*=2, LLaMA-8B K*=2, Pythia-1.4B K*=1. Three additional models (Mistral-7B, Qwen-7B, Qwen-14B) are marked \"---\" in Table 5, meaning untested. At the time of writing, two experiments are still running (exp_code_mistral7b, exp_code_qwen7b). A 2/3 split (or 2/5 if pending results come in mixed) does not support \"Code ceiling = 2\" as a robust claim. The \"domain ceiling + model competence\" framework is post-hoc: any deviation from the hierarchy is explained as \"insufficient model competence,\" making the framework unfalsifiable.\n\n**W6. Comparison to Drayson et al. (2025) does not establish clear incremental value.**\n\nDrayson et al. (2025) already combine binary MGT detection with importance resampling for collapse prevention, achieving SIR filtering that \"not only prevents collapse but improves over human-only training.\" The paper's own C3 results show that binary filtering is all you need, and graduated (depth-aware) filtering adds nothing. The paper positions Drayson as \"a special case when K*=1,\" but since K*=2 only adds a distinction between depth-0 and depth-1+ (which is binary), and graduated weighting beyond that is useless, the practical increment over Drayson is nil. The characterization contribution (measuring K*) stands independently, but the curation motivation collapses.\n\n**W7. The \"one-wash laundering\" framing overstates the finding.**\n\n\"One-wash laundering\" (a single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content) is a catchy concept, but it describes the K*=1 case (Wikipedia, Mistral on C4), not the K*=2 majority. For K*=2, depth-1 IS distinguishable from depth-2; it is depth-2 that becomes indistinguishable from depth-3+. This is \"two-wash,\" not \"one-wash.\" The Abstract states \"a single additional generation pass suffices to render first-generation synthetic web text indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content,\" which is technically about the 1v2 vs. 2v3 transition, but the \"one-wash\" branding suggests something more dramatic than the data support.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On novelty:** Given that model collapse theory already predicts rapid convergence within a few iterations, what specific new knowledge does K* <= 3 provide that was not implicit in Shumailov et al. (2024) or Dohmatob et al. (2024)? Can you articulate a concrete prediction or decision that changes based on knowing K*=2 vs. knowing \"recursive generation converges fast\"?\n\n2. **On practical utility:** Since graduated filtering provides zero benefit everywhere, and binary filtering provides marginal benefit (0.76pp MMLU, and only on Qwen), what is the practical use case for K*? If someone were building a data curation pipeline today, what would they do differently with this paper versus simply using Drayson et al.'s binary filtering?\n\n3. **On threshold robustness:** The paper reports K*=2 as the headline for C4, but at theta=0.625 (just 2.5pp above the chosen threshold), some configurations begin dropping to K*=1. At theta=0.65, roughly half drop. How do you justify theta=0.60 as the \"right\" threshold rather than, say, 0.625 or 0.65? The LOFO validation recovers theta in [0.5725, 0.6004], but the upper bound (0.6004) barely includes 0.60.\n\n4. **On the domain ceiling framework:** Pythia-1.4B achieves K*=1 on Code (explained as \"insufficient code competence\") and Mistral-7B achieves K*=1 on C4 (explained as \"compressed surprisal range\"). These are different mechanisms. Is \"domain ceiling + model competence\" a genuine framework or a post-hoc rationalization that can explain any outcome? What observation would falsify the domain ceiling claim?\n\n5. **On scale:** The paper shows 0v1 AUC declining with scale (>0.99 at 1.5B, 0.922 at 8B). At 32B, preliminary data (exp_32b_c4, still running) may show further decline. If K* decreases to 1 at 32B+, would that invalidate the K*=2 headline, or would the paper reframe as \"K*=2 for small models\"?\n\n6. **On the retrain-chain divergence:** Retrain-chain K*=7 vs. prompt-chain K*=2 is a 3.5x difference. This suggests K* is highly protocol-dependent. In real-world contamination (which involves both prompting and retraining), what is K*? The paper positions prompt-chain as \"conservative,\" but in practice, the contamination mechanism is unknown. How useful is a K* measured under one protocol when the real-world mechanism is different?\n\n7. **On ordinal accuracy:** The paper reports 38--42% 6-class ordinal accuracy (Table S3), with frontier LLMs at 33%. For practical depth estimation (as opposed to binary detection), this means the classifier is wrong 58--62% of the time. Is \"depth estimation\" an accurate description of what the system does, given this performance level?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The Conclusion is a single sentence. This is unusually terse even for a page-constrained submission and feels incomplete.\n- Table 5 (Domain x Model K* matrix) has three \"---\" entries for Code. Submitting with incomplete data weakens the claim.\n- The paper uses \"largely generator-independent\" repeatedly, but the qualification \"largely\" does important work: Qwen-cont drops under cross-scoring, Mistral is K*=1, rewrite mode transfers at only 61--63%. The hedging is appropriate but the Abstract may overstate generalizability.\n- Cohen's h = 0.20 is cited as motivation for theta = 0.60, but AUC = 0.60 does not directly correspond to Cohen's h = 0.20; the mapping depends on the score distributions. This justification is hand-wavy.\n- The ANOVA in Table (Appendix) has correlated residuals (per-fold AUC values share seed documents), which the paper acknowledges but still reports p-values for. The effect-size interpretation is more appropriate, but then the ANOVA machinery adds little beyond a simpler variance decomposition.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject / Findings-level)**\n\nThe paper is a thorough, honest, and well-executed empirical study. The experimental coverage is impressive, the ablations are extensive, and the transparency about negative results is commendable. However, the core contribution faces a novelty-significance dilemma:\n\n- The **characterization contribution** (K* <= 3, domain hierarchy) is incremental over what model collapse theory already implies. Rapid convergence of recursive generation is a well-established phenomenon; quantifying it with a specific classifier and threshold, while useful, does not yield a surprising or actionable insight.\n- The **practical contribution** (C3, filtering) is negative by the paper's own admission. Graduated filtering adds nothing; binary filtering's advantage is marginal and was already demonstrated by Drayson et al. (2025). K* provides no actionable information beyond \"use binary detection.\"\n- The **methodological contribution** (15D features + LightGBM) is standard.\n- The **headline result** (K*=2 for web text) is fragile: threshold-sensitive, scorer-sensitive, sample-size-sensitive, and potentially scale-dependent.\n\nThe paper would benefit from either (a) a stronger theoretical contribution (e.g., deriving K* bounds from model/domain properties rather than just invoking DPI), (b) a positive downstream application where K* provides genuine value beyond binary detection, or (c) framing as a pure measurement/negative-result paper without the curation motivation that the experiments do not support.\n\nFor EMNLP 2026 Main (acceptance rate ~25%), the novelty threshold is not met. The paper may be suitable for Findings if the framing is adjusted to center the empirical measurement and negative results as the primary contribution, rather than positioning K* as practically useful for data governance.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4 / 5.** I have read the full paper including all appendices, verified the experimental claims against the reported numbers, and am familiar with the model collapse, MGT detection, and data curation literatures. My main uncertainty is whether the community would value the systematic measurement more than I do; the comprehensiveness of the experiments is genuinely unusual and may carry weight with other reviewers."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a89a647112aada867 (use SendMessage with to: 'a89a647112aada867' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 104203\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 163547</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4ae90b1b-19b4-4021-a8d9-15784d0863dc","uuid":"29964fd2-720d-4e24-b283-24799537bc7f","timestamp":"2026-05-19T10:14:00.510Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus areas are **novelty/significance** and **related work positioning**. Evaluate against a top ML venue standard (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Bounded Generational Discrimination in Recursive Synthetic Text: K* Saturates Within Three Passes\"\n\nThe paper introduces *generational discrimination depth* K*, defined as the maximum generation depth at which pairwise statistical discrimination (via AUC of a LightGBM classifier on 15D text features) remains reliable above threshold θ=0.60. DPI guarantees K* is finite. Core finding: K* ∈ {1,2,3} across all tested conditions.\n\n**Three claimed contributions:**\n1. (C1) Formalize K* with DPI grounding + first systematic measurement across 9 model configs, 4 domains, 4 decoding strategies → K* ∈ {1,2,3}\n2. (C2) Domain hierarchy: Wikipedia K*=1, C4/Code K*∈{1,2}, arXiv K*=3\n3. (C3) Downstream K*-aware filtering: binary helps (+0.76pp MMLU), graduated depth-aware = zero benefit\n\n## Related Work as Presented\n\nThe paper covers three areas:\n1. **Model Collapse & Recursive Generation:** Shumailov et al. 2024 (Nature), Alemohammad et al. 2024 (ICLR), Dohmatob et al. 2024 (ICML). Paper claims it bridges the gap by \"operationalizing depth discrimination and measuring where it saturates.\"\n2. **Machine-Generated Text Detection:** DetectGPT (Mitchell 2023), DivEye, Binoculars, Min-K%++. Drayson et al. 2025 (binary detection for data curation). Paper's 15D features extend beyond binary to ordinal depth estimation.\n3. **Data Selection/Curation:** DSIR (Xie et al. 2023), D4, DoReMi.\n4. **Information Theory:** DPI from Cover & Thomas 2006.\n\nTotal references: only 7 cited papers.\n\n## Key Results to Consider for Novelty Assessment\n\n**Domain hierarchy:**\n- Wikipedia: K*=1 (all 6 models)\n- C4: K*=2 (8/9 configs)\n- arXiv: K*=3 (5/5)\n- Code: K*=2 for 2/5, K*=1 for 3/5 (model-dependent, no clean ceiling)\n\n**θ-sensitivity destroys the \"clean\" hierarchy:**\n- θ=0.60: 8/9 C4 at K*=2\n- θ=0.65: ~5/9 C4 at K*=2\n- θ=0.70: ~2/9 C4 at K*=2\n\n**C3 (downstream filtering) is entirely negative:**\n- Binary > no-filter by 0.76pp MMLU (Qwen-1.5B on C4 only)\n- Graduated = no-filter at ALL contamination levels (10/30/50%)\n- Pythia replication: negative (all strategies within noise, Δ_max=0.17pp)\n- arXiv filtering: negative despite K*=3 (all strategies identical)\n- All CPT conditions degrade vs base model (0.6018 MMLU)\n\n**Other key findings:**\n- Cross-model transfer: binary (0v1) transfers perfectly (>0.96 AUC), but cross-model average pairwise AUC for depth = 0.5905, below θ\n- Neural probe: Qwen K*_neural=3 > K*_15D=2, suggesting 15D features are a lossy bottleneck\n- Retrain chain K*=7 vs prompt chain K*=2 → mechanism dependence\n- Scale trend: 0v1 AUC declining with scale (1.5B→32B), 32B borderline K*=1-2\n- 512-token text: borderline CI failure\n- OBD vs frontier LLMs: McNemar p=0.081/0.069, not significant\n- Scorer confound: Wikipedia same-family inflates K* by +1; arXiv cross-scorer preservation 75%\n- Ordinal accuracy: 38-42% (6-class), far from practical utility\n- Sample size requirement: n=2000 minimum for reliable K*=2 detection\n\n**Paper's own honest admissions:**\n- \"graduated depth-aware weighting provides zero additional benefit over binary filtering at any contamination level tested\"\n- \"the negative result for depth-aware curation does not diminish the characterization contribution\"\n- \"this negative result... is an important negative result for the field\"\n- Scale limitation acknowledged\n- Naturalistic validation (Common Crawl) not done\n- Scorer confound coverage incomplete\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProduce a structured review covering:\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with specific evidence)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score 1-6 with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nFocus on:\n- **Is K*≤3 a surprising finding?** Model collapse literature (Shumailov, Alemohammad, Dohmatob) already establishes rapid convergence. Does this paper add genuine new knowledge or just quantify what's implicitly known?\n- **Is the DPI argument a real theoretical contribution or a trivial observation?** DPI says K* is finite, but that's obvious for any lossy channel. The paper provides no upper bound from theory.\n- **Related work coverage:** Only 7 references. Missing: watermarking literature (Kirchenbauer et al. 2023), perplexity-based detection (GPTZero, etc.), data contamination benchmarks, model collapse theory (Bertrand et al. 2024, Gerstgrasser et al. 2024), paraphrase detection, training data extraction. Is this adequate for a top venue?\n- **Does the complete C3 failure undermine the paper's motivation?** Introduction frames K* as relevant to \"data governance.\" If K* has no downstream utility, what's the practical significance?\n- **Novelty of 15D features:** Surprisal, entropy, distributional stats are standard text quality metrics used in MGT detection since 2023. Is this a methodological contribution?\n- **Is the domain hierarchy robust?** Code domain is model-dependent (2/5 K*=2), C4 is θ-dependent. Only Wikipedia (K*=1) and arXiv (K*=3) show clean results. Is \"messy middle\" a contribution?\n- **How does this compare to Drayson et al. 2025?** That paper already does binary detection for curation. This paper's C3 shows binary is all you need. What's the increment?\n- **Paper honesty vs. impact:** The paper is commendably honest about negative results, but does honesty about limitations substitute for actual positive contributions?\n\nBe thorough and critical but fair. Cite specific data from the paper.","agentId":"a89a647112aada867","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text: Measuring the Discrimination Boundary\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Focus: Novelty/Significance and Related Work Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper formalizes the concept of a generational discrimination boundary K* (the maximum recursive generation depth at which adjacent generations remain statistically distinguishable) and measures it empirically using 15 hand-crafted statistical features and LightGBM classifiers. Across 9 model configurations (5 architecture families, 1B--14B), 4 domains, and 4 decoding strategies, the paper finds K* in {1, 2, 3}, forming a domain hierarchy: Wikipedia K*=1, C4/Code K*=2, arXiv K*=3. A downstream filtering experiment shows binary filtering marginally outperforms graduated depth-aware weighting, which the authors interpret as consistent with K*=2 predicting binary granularity as optimal. The paper is thorough in its ablations and commendably transparent about negative results and boundary fragility.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Systematic empirical coverage.** The experimental matrix is impressively comprehensive: 9 model configurations, 4 domains, 4 decoding strategies, cross-scorer validation, neural probes, retrain-chain comparisons, dose-response analysis, feature ablations, and power analysis. This is one of the most thorough empirical studies I have seen on recursive generation phenomena. The 77+ experiments reflect genuine scientific diligence.\n\n- **Intellectual honesty.** The paper is unusually forthright about negative results: graduated filtering provides zero benefit, cross-scorer validation drops one configuration below threshold, the Pythia replication shows negligible effects, arXiv filtering is negative, Code domain shows model-dependent variation, and 512-token segments fail CI tests. This honesty strengthens credibility and is rare in submitted manuscripts.\n\n- **Well-defined operational metric.** The formalization of K* via Eq. 1 (CI-based threshold criterion) is clean and reproducible. The leave-one-family-out threshold validation (Table in Appendix) is a thoughtful methodological addition that partially addresses threshold arbitrariness concerns.\n\n- **Domain hierarchy as organizing principle.** The finding that domain structural complexity (Wikipedia < C4/Code < arXiv) governs K* more than model identity or scale is a useful empirical observation. The Mistral C4 K*=1 vs. arXiv K*=3 contrast is the paper's most compelling data point for domain dependence.\n\n- **Cross-scorer validation.** The ANOVA decomposition (Table in Appendix) showing Depth-Pair dominates variance (eta-squared = 0.998) while Scorer is minor (0.192) is a well-designed control that addresses a real confound.\n\n- **Related work is now reasonably comprehensive.** The bibliography contains approximately 40 references covering model collapse, binary detection, data selection, ordinal classification, watermarking, paraphrase robustness, and beyond-binary detection. This is adequate for the scope.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The core finding (K* <= 3) has limited novelty beyond what model collapse theory already implies.**\n\nThe model collapse literature (Shumailov et al. 2024, Dohmatob et al. 2024, Alemohammad et al. 2024) already establishes that recursive generation converges rapidly, with tail modes vanishing within a few iterations. Kazdan et al. (2025) show covariance collapse to zero within 5--10 iterations in the replace regime. Shi et al. (2025) show GS drops approximately exponentially. The paper's own JSD analysis (Figure in Discussion) shows the same exponential decay pattern. Given this prior knowledge, the finding that a 15D LightGBM classifier cannot distinguish generations beyond depth 2--3 is not surprising; it would be surprising if K* were large. The DPI argument is invoked as \"theoretical grounding,\" but DPI merely guarantees monotonic information loss in any Markov chain, which is trivially true. The paper acknowledges this (\"DPI guarantees K* is finite\") but does not derive any non-trivial bound from theory. The gap between \"DPI says K* exists\" and \"we measure K* = 2\" is filled entirely by empirical measurement, not by theory.\n\n**W2. The downstream utility of K* is essentially zero, undermining the practical motivation.**\n\nThe paper's Introduction frames K* as relevant to data curation: \"the saturation point determines filtering granularity.\" However:\n- Graduated filtering provides zero benefit over binary at ALL tested conditions (C4, arXiv, all contamination levels 10--50%, both Qwen and Pythia). This is the paper's own finding, stated explicitly.\n- Binary filtering's advantage over no-filter is 0.76pp MMLU on Qwen (Table in Section 5). On Pythia, the advantage is 0.17pp MMLU, within noise.\n- All CPT conditions degrade vs. the base model (base MMLU = 0.6018; best filtered = 0.5941). The filtering does not recover base model performance.\n- ArXiv filtering (where K*=3 should matter most) shows no benefit for any strategy.\n- The \"K* predicts optimal granularity\" claim reduces to: \"binary is better than graduated\" (true) and \"binary is better than nothing\" (marginally true, 0.76pp). But binary detection for data curation was already demonstrated by Drayson et al. (2025). K* adds no actionable information beyond what binary detection already provides.\n\n**W3. The 15D feature set is standard and the classifier is off-the-shelf; there is no methodological contribution.**\n\nThe 15 features (surprisal moments, derivatives, TTR, hapax ratio, self-BLEU, frequency statistics) are well-known text quality metrics used in MGT detection since at least 2019 (GLTR). DivEye shares 4 of these features. The classifier is LightGBM with default-like hyperparameters. The OBD decomposition follows Frank & Hall (2001). The paper correctly frames its contribution as \"problem formulation + empirical characterization\" rather than methodology, but this means the technical novelty rests entirely on the K* concept and its measurement, which loops back to W1.\n\n**W4. K*=2 is fragile and threshold-dependent, weakening the headline claim.**\n\nThe headline \"K*=2 for web text\" is sensitive to multiple factors:\n- **Threshold sensitivity:** At theta=0.65, roughly 5/9 C4 configurations retain K*=2; at theta=0.70, all drop to K*=1 (Figure 3). The stable window is only [0.575, 0.625], a 5pp range.\n- **Cross-scorer sensitivity:** Qwen-cont drops from K*=2 to K*=1 under cross-family scoring (Table in Appendix). The CI lower bound exceeds theta by only 0.2pp (0.602 vs. 0.600).\n- **Sample size sensitivity:** At n=2200 (matching arXiv), C4 drops to K*=1 (Appendix).\n- **Length sensitivity:** 512-token segments produce CI failure (lower bound 0.5979 < 0.60).\n- **Scale trend:** 0v1 AUC declines from >0.99 at 1.5B to 0.922 at 8B, suggesting K* may decrease further at larger scales. The paper acknowledges this but has no data beyond 14B.\n\nThe boundary between K*=1 and K*=2 for web text is essentially a coin flip at the margins. Many of the \"K*=2\" assignments survive by 0.2--2.8pp above theta. This fragility undermines the claim that K*=2 is a robust, generalizable finding rather than a measurement artifact of the specific theta, sample size, and feature set.\n\n**W5. The Code domain results are incomplete and weaken the claimed hierarchy.**\n\nThe domain hierarchy (Wiki=1, C4=Code=2, arXiv=3) is clean only for Wikipedia and arXiv. Code shows: Qwen-1.5B K*=2, LLaMA-8B K*=2, Pythia-1.4B K*=1. Three additional models (Mistral-7B, Qwen-7B, Qwen-14B) are marked \"---\" in Table 5, meaning untested. At the time of writing, two experiments are still running (exp_code_mistral7b, exp_code_qwen7b). A 2/3 split (or 2/5 if pending results come in mixed) does not support \"Code ceiling = 2\" as a robust claim. The \"domain ceiling + model competence\" framework is post-hoc: any deviation from the hierarchy is explained as \"insufficient model competence,\" making the framework unfalsifiable.\n\n**W6. Comparison to Drayson et al. (2025) does not establish clear incremental value.**\n\nDrayson et al. (2025) already combine binary MGT detection with importance resampling for collapse prevention, achieving SIR filtering that \"not only prevents collapse but improves over human-only training.\" The paper's own C3 results show that binary filtering is all you need, and graduated (depth-aware) filtering adds nothing. The paper positions Drayson as \"a special case when K*=1,\" but since K*=2 only adds a distinction between depth-0 and depth-1+ (which is binary), and graduated weighting beyond that is useless, the practical increment over Drayson is nil. The characterization contribution (measuring K*) stands independently, but the curation motivation collapses.\n\n**W7. The \"one-wash laundering\" framing overstates the finding.**\n\n\"One-wash laundering\" (a single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content) is a catchy concept, but it describes the K*=1 case (Wikipedia, Mistral on C4), not the K*=2 majority. For K*=2, depth-1 IS distinguishable from depth-2; it is depth-2 that becomes indistinguishable from depth-3+. This is \"two-wash,\" not \"one-wash.\" The Abstract states \"a single additional generation pass suffices to render first-generation synthetic web text indistinguishable from deeper synthetic content,\" which is technically about the 1v2 vs. 2v3 transition, but the \"one-wash\" branding suggests something more dramatic than the data support.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On novelty:** Given that model collapse theory already predicts rapid convergence within a few iterations, what specific new knowledge does K* <= 3 provide that was not implicit in Shumailov et al. (2024) or Dohmatob et al. (2024)? Can you articulate a concrete prediction or decision that changes based on knowing K*=2 vs. knowing \"recursive generation converges fast\"?\n\n2. **On practical utility:** Since graduated filtering provides zero benefit everywhere, and binary filtering provides marginal benefit (0.76pp MMLU, and only on Qwen), what is the practical use case for K*? If someone were building a data curation pipeline today, what would they do differently with this paper versus simply using Drayson et al.'s binary filtering?\n\n3. **On threshold robustness:** The paper reports K*=2 as the headline for C4, but at theta=0.625 (just 2.5pp above the chosen threshold), some configurations begin dropping to K*=1. At theta=0.65, roughly half drop. How do you justify theta=0.60 as the \"right\" threshold rather than, say, 0.625 or 0.65? The LOFO validation recovers theta in [0.5725, 0.6004], but the upper bound (0.6004) barely includes 0.60.\n\n4. **On the domain ceiling framework:** Pythia-1.4B achieves K*=1 on Code (explained as \"insufficient code competence\") and Mistral-7B achieves K*=1 on C4 (explained as \"compressed surprisal range\"). These are different mechanisms. Is \"domain ceiling + model competence\" a genuine framework or a post-hoc rationalization that can explain any outcome? What observation would falsify the domain ceiling claim?\n\n5. **On scale:** The paper shows 0v1 AUC declining with scale (>0.99 at 1.5B, 0.922 at 8B). At 32B, preliminary data (exp_32b_c4, still running) may show further decline. If K* decreases to 1 at 32B+, would that invalidate the K*=2 headline, or would the paper reframe as \"K*=2 for small models\"?\n\n6. **On the retrain-chain divergence:** Retrain-chain K*=7 vs. prompt-chain K*=2 is a 3.5x difference. This suggests K* is highly protocol-dependent. In real-world contamination (which involves both prompting and retraining), what is K*? The paper positions prompt-chain as \"conservative,\" but in practice, the contamination mechanism is unknown. How useful is a K* measured under one protocol when the real-world mechanism is different?\n\n7. **On ordinal accuracy:** The paper reports 38--42% 6-class ordinal accuracy (Table S3), with frontier LLMs at 33%. For practical depth estimation (as opposed to binary detection), this means the classifier is wrong 58--62% of the time. Is \"depth estimation\" an accurate description of what the system does, given this performance level?\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The Conclusion is a single sentence. This is unusually terse even for a page-constrained submission and feels incomplete.\n- Table 5 (Domain x Model K* matrix) has three \"---\" entries for Code. Submitting with incomplete data weakens the claim.\n- The paper uses \"largely generator-independent\" repeatedly, but the qualification \"largely\" does important work: Qwen-cont drops under cross-scoring, Mistral is K*=1, rewrite mode transfers at only 61--63%. The hedging is appropriate but the Abstract may overstate generalizability.\n- Cohen's h = 0.20 is cited as motivation for theta = 0.60, but AUC = 0.60 does not directly correspond to Cohen's h = 0.20; the mapping depends on the score distributions. This justification is hand-wavy.\n- The ANOVA in Table (Appendix) has correlated residuals (per-fold AUC values share seed documents), which the paper acknowledges but still reports p-values for. The effect-size interpretation is more appropriate, but then the ANOVA machinery adds little beyond a simpler variance decomposition.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject / Findings-level)**\n\nThe paper is a thorough, honest, and well-executed empirical study. The experimental coverage is impressive, the ablations are extensive, and the transparency about negative results is commendable. However, the core contribution faces a novelty-significance dilemma:\n\n- The **characterization contribution** (K* <= 3, domain hierarchy) is incremental over what model collapse theory already implies. Rapid convergence of recursive generation is a well-established phenomenon; quantifying it with a specific classifier and threshold, while useful, does not yield a surprising or actionable insight.\n- The **practical contribution** (C3, filtering) is negative by the paper's own admission. Graduated filtering adds nothing; binary filtering's advantage is marginal and was already demonstrated by Drayson et al. (2025). K* provides no actionable information beyond \"use binary detection.\"\n- The **methodological contribution** (15D features + LightGBM) is standard.\n- The **headline result** (K*=2 for web text) is fragile: threshold-sensitive, scorer-sensitive, sample-size-sensitive, and potentially scale-dependent.\n\nThe paper would benefit from either (a) a stronger theoretical contribution (e.g., deriving K* bounds from model/domain properties rather than just invoking DPI), (b) a positive downstream application where K* provides genuine value beyond binary detection, or (c) framing as a pure measurement/negative-result paper without the curation motivation that the experiments do not support.\n\nFor EMNLP 2026 Main (acceptance rate ~25%), the novelty threshold is not met. The paper may be suitable for Findings if the framing is adjusted to center the empirical measurement and negative results as the primary contribution, rather than positioning K* as practically useful for data governance.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**4 / 5.** I have read the full paper including all appendices, verified the experimental claims against the reported numbers, and am familiar with the model collapse, MGT detection, and data curation literatures. My main uncertainty is whether the community would value the systematic measurement more than I do; the comprehensiveness of the experiments is genuinely unusual and may carry weight with other reviewers."}],"totalDurationMs":163547,"totalTokens":104203,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":687,"cache_read_input_tokens":99464,"output_tokens":4049,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":687},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4049,"cache_read_input_tokens":99464,"cache_creation_input_tokens":687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":687,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
